// Emission happens only if every check passed.
//
// The prune is here, between the last check and the first line of output, and
// that position is the contract: every rule of the language has been checked
// over every function by now, so throwing the unreachable ones away cannot
// stop anything from being checked. See dce.id.
guarded_emit(int argc, string[] argv) {
  if(check_failed() == 0) {
    guarded_prune();
    init_tyc();
    emit(argc, argv);
  }
} return void;

// A freestanding object keeps every function, exactly as a project with no
// main does, and for the same reason: it is not the whole program. Something
// outside it -- a boot stub, an interrupt vector, another object -- may be the
// only caller, and reachability from `main` cannot see that. It saw it wrongly
// once: the kernel's fault handler is called from the interrupt stubs and
// nothing else, so it was pruned and the link failed on a symbol the program
// obviously needed. Deciding what is live across a link is the linker's job.
guarded_prune() {
  if(is_freestanding() == 0) {
    dce_prune();
  }
} return void;
